// Exercise 3 (Swift track): what makes the real cbindgen header importable
// as a Swift module.
//
// This file is the whole reason this track differs from the other three.
// Python's cffi reads the header at runtime, Dart and Kotlin/JNA don't read
// it at all; Swift hands it to clang, which turns it into a module, and
// `import AocDay` then puts `aoc_2024_12_03_part1` in scope as a normal
// typed function. There is no hand-transcribed signature anywhere in
// solve.swift, and a header that stopped matching would be a compile error
// rather than a runtime surprise.
//
// The header path is relative to this file, and the header does not exist
// on a fresh clone (.gitignore) — `just days swift-demo 2024-12-03`
// regenerates it first. `[system]` is the standard marker for a C header
// you don't own: it silences the warnings clang would otherwise raise
// inside it, which is right for generated code nobody is going to edit.
module AocDay [system] {
    header "../include/aoc_2024_12_03.h"
    export *
}
